                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "3b.  $ \\underline{\\text{Many retailers}} $ have centralized functions over the past several years, leading customers to object to more complicated purchasing procedures.",
            "block_bbox": [
                127,
                945,
                753,
                996
            ],
            "block_id": 13,
            "block_order": 12,
            "group_id": 13,
            "global_block_id": 13,
            "global_group_id": 13,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    127.0,
                    945.0
                ],
                [
                    753.0,
                    945.0
                ],
                [
                    753.0,
                    996.0
                ],
                [
                    127.0,
                    996.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "Most readers prefer 3b to 3a. One reason is that in 3b the simple subject is a character, retailers, but another is that the whole subject is short, only two words. In 3a, the main character retailers is present in the whole subject, buried in a prepositional phrase, but the simple subject is the nominalization centralization. The whole subject is also a sprawling twelve words long. That's a problem, because readers get impatient when a subject goes on for more than a handful of words. They like to get quickly to the verb and into the back of the sentence. Notice also that in 3a, ten words come between the simple subject and the verb; in 3b, the",
            "block_bbox": [
